-`color_source = "window"` paints the bar with the top 5 logical pixels of the window's own content instead of `background`, so the bar takes on the application's colour and follows it as the application repaints that edge. A window whose top edge is transparent keeps the configured `background` there, because the bar colour stays painted underneath, and the values in `[colors.title_bar]` remain the fallback for applications that draw nothing. Unfocused windows keep their own colour, dimmed over `background_unfocused`. Because the bar can be any colour the application paints, the title and the button symbols carry a dark edge, so they stay readable on light and dark windows alike. The bar mirrors the window's own buffer instead of reading pixels back, so it costs no extra work per frame beyond drawing that strip.
+`color_source = "window"` paints the bar with the top 5 logical pixels of the window's own content instead of `background`, so the bar takes on the application's colour and follows it as the application repaints that edge. A window whose top edge is transparent keeps the configured `background` there, because the bar colour stays painted underneath, and the values in `[colors.title_bar]` remain the fallback for applications that draw nothing. Unfocused windows keep their own colour, dimmed over `background_unfocused`. Because the bar can be any colour the application paints, the title and the button symbols carry a dark edge, so they stay readable on light and dark windows alike. A client-drawn window that reports its own geometry keeps the transparent shadows around it out of the colour, so the bar follows the visible window rather than its padding. The bar mirrors the window's own buffer instead of reading pixels back: it adds one bar-sized rectangle per window and a copy of that strip each time the window commits, not a per-frame readback.
 
